WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.703
-Our first guest tonight is an
Emmy and Peabody award-winning

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:06.806
actress and comedian you know
from movies such as "Trainwreck"

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:08.775
and her show
"Inside Amy Schumer."

00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:10.309
Her latest series,
"Life &amp; Beth,"

00:00:10.309 --> 00:00:11.778
is streaming now on Hulu.

00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:13.112
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.112 --> 00:00:16.415
-Do you have any preexisting
conditions?

00:00:16.415 --> 00:00:17.783
-I'm a woman.

00:00:17.783 --> 00:00:20.787
-A woman, mm-hmm.

00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:22.888
Uh, just a few more questions.

00:00:22.888 --> 00:00:25.959
-I can just save you some time.

00:00:25.959 --> 00:00:27.894
I've had over 30,000 drinks.

00:00:27.894 --> 00:00:30.497
I don't really chew my food
enough.

00:00:30.497 --> 00:00:33.365
And I took Plan B recently.

00:00:33.365 --> 00:00:36.068
Kind of got two uses out of it.

00:00:36.068 --> 00:00:37.237
Don't write that.

00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:40.640
-Hmm, I just meant
like any injuries.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:41.841
-Right.

00:00:41.841 --> 00:00:43.342
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:43.342 --> 00:00:45.144
our good friend Amy Schumer,
everybody!

00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:46.979
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:46.979 --> 00:00:56.255 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:56.255 --> 00:01:05.565 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:09.502
Amy, how are you?!

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:13.305
-I mean, I'm [bleep] depressed.
-Why?

00:01:13.305 --> 00:01:15.841
-Just, I mean,
it's not a big deal.

00:01:15.841 --> 00:01:17.443
Just that we're losing
all of our rights.

00:01:17.443 --> 00:01:18.611
-Yeah, I'm sorry about that.

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:20.713
-Yeah, but whatever.
What else is going on?

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:23.616
No, but I'm wearing my
"bans off our bodies" pin.

00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:24.717
-And you were at a protest
yesterday.

00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:26.352
-I was at a protest yesterday.

00:01:26.352 --> 00:01:28.921
Yeah, we really need everybody
to be standing up right now.

00:01:28.921 --> 00:01:31.891
Men, women, everybody, yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:34.126
-You've been very good at that.
I want to give you credit.

00:01:34.126 --> 00:01:36.962
-Sorry, I cursed,
but, God, it's such a bummer!

00:01:36.962 --> 00:01:38.731
-It is not
a great time right now.

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:40.767
-It's not good.
-Can one thing that --

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:43.336
Well, I guess this is good.

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:46.639
You are one of the people,
one of the few I know,

00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:50.609
over the last 26 months,
has not gotten COVID.

00:01:50.609 --> 00:01:53.046
-I know!

00:01:53.046 --> 00:01:54.647
[ Laughter ]

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:56.215
You had COVID.
-I did.

00:01:56.215 --> 00:01:58.284
-Yeah, I didn't. I haven't.
-I know.

00:01:58.284 --> 00:01:59.385
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:01.387
-Has anybody else
not had COVID yet?

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:03.989
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:03.989 --> 00:02:06.359
Look how smug. "Yeah."

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:08.528
[ Laughter ]
I don't know, God has a plan.

00:02:08.528 --> 00:02:09.695
[ Laughter ]
I don't know.

00:02:09.695 --> 00:02:11.631
He chooses the people He loves.

00:02:11.631 --> 00:02:13.800
I don't know.
-That's what you think it is?

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.501
-I don't know.
I'm a little smug about it.

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:17.837
I haven't had it yet.
-Well, congratulations.

00:02:17.837 --> 00:02:19.372
-Hey, He's looking out.

00:02:19.372 --> 00:02:21.774
[ Laughter ]
So stupid.

00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:24.310
-I know you've said
this to me in the past.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:27.012
As the weather changes,
you're very excited to go out,

00:02:27.012 --> 00:02:30.015
have drinks outside.
-Yes, I love drinking outside

00:02:30.015 --> 00:02:31.184
when it gets nice out.

00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:32.552
Isn't that nice, you know?
-Yeah.

00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:33.986
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:33.986 --> 00:02:38.691
-But then also when it's not
nice out, having a drink.

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:40.260
Isn't that good?
-Okay.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.061
-You know, even it's like
the kind of thing, like,

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:44.296
when it's, like,
snowing out in New York,

00:02:44.296 --> 00:02:45.932
you want a little red wine.
-Yeah!

00:02:45.932 --> 00:02:48.801
-And then, when it's spring
and it's beautiful,

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:50.436
you want that red wine.

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:51.604
[ Laughter ]
You know?

00:02:51.604 --> 00:02:54.974
-So is that...?
-Like, this is the kind of

00:02:54.974 --> 00:02:56.409
drinking I've been doing lately.
-Okay.

00:02:56.409 --> 00:02:58.544
-Okay, just to give you
sort of a frame of reference.

00:02:58.544 --> 00:03:00.713
Like, I've been drinking
so much, like,

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:04.550
last night is the only night
I didn't drink, and I drank.

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.519
Do you know what I mean?
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:08.188
-Okay, yeah.
-So, even you're, like,

00:03:08.188 --> 00:03:10.857
your break day --
-My nights off are on.

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:12.091
-You have, like, a little.
-Yeah.

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:14.594
Like, have you ever been
so hungover,

00:03:14.594 --> 00:03:17.063
you just have
to, like, leave town?

00:03:17.063 --> 00:03:18.598
[ Laughter ]

00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:21.134
You're just, like, you don't
even know where you're going.

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:24.203
You're just kind of shaking,
packing toiletries, you know?

00:03:24.203 --> 00:03:25.704
[ Laughter ]
No?

00:03:25.704 --> 00:03:29.008
-Do you know, is this,
like, a blackout situation?

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:33.145
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
Usually after a blackout.

00:03:33.145 --> 00:03:35.081
I don't black any out any more,
I don't think.

00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:36.749
-You don't? That's good. Okay.

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:38.150
[ Laughter ]

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:40.853
-No, because, you know,
it used to be cool to black out.

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:42.188
It would be like,
"I blacked out,"

00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:44.357
and everyone's like,
"you're the best," you know?

00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:47.126
But really, nothing good
ever happens in a blackout.

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:48.360
Like, I've never woken up like,

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.496
"What is this pilates mat
doing out?"

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:52.297
You know?

00:03:52.297 --> 00:03:55.568
"Oh, my God, I posted a
thoughtful James Baldwin quote?"

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:58.271
Like, that's not --
that's not really how it goes.

00:03:58.271 --> 00:03:59.905
-We were both out very late.

00:03:59.905 --> 00:04:01.774
It was very late for me
on Monday.

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:03.308
Met Ball.
-Whoa-oh, model!

00:04:03.308 --> 00:04:07.379
-There you go, the Met ball.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:07.379 --> 00:04:09.849
This is a beautiful photo.

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:12.284
You took a couple of
really nice groupies, as well.

00:04:12.284 --> 00:04:14.053
-Oh, yeah.
-Group photos.

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:16.222
-Look at the Hadid sister,
right there.

00:04:16.222 --> 00:04:18.023
Look at that.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:18.023 --> 00:04:19.258
-And there she is, there.

00:04:19.258 --> 00:04:20.927
I like how you showed,
"I can be with people

00:04:20.927 --> 00:04:22.528
who also have sunglasses
and people who don't."

00:04:22.528 --> 00:04:23.729
-And people
who don't have sunglasses.

00:04:23.729 --> 00:04:25.264
I can still be with them,
as well.

00:04:25.264 --> 00:04:27.433
-Yeah, very nice.
-Look at these hags.

00:04:27.433 --> 00:04:28.767
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:28.767 --> 00:04:30.703
I mean, it's not a contest,
but it is, really.

00:04:30.703 --> 00:04:32.204
-But I am winning.

00:04:32.204 --> 00:04:33.939
-You're going to frame this and
call it "Amy and Three Hags."

00:04:33.939 --> 00:04:35.475
-Well, that's what it is.

00:04:35.475 --> 00:04:38.211
[ Laughter ]

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:40.246
-There were a lot of -
I mean, you know,

00:04:40.246 --> 00:04:42.948
there were a lot of people who I
think are most famous

00:04:42.948 --> 00:04:45.017
for their work on reality shows
at the Met.

00:04:45.017 --> 00:04:46.753
Are you a fan
of reality shows yourself?

00:04:46.753 --> 00:04:49.522
-Oh, my God, am I ever.
-Are you really?

00:04:49.522 --> 00:04:50.689
-Of course, yeah.

00:04:50.689 --> 00:04:53.192
I watch, like,
every reality show,

00:04:53.192 --> 00:04:55.427
and it's hard,
but I find the time.

00:04:55.427 --> 00:04:57.630
-That's really good.
-Have you been watching --

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.666
does anybody watch
"Selling Sunset"?

00:04:59.666 --> 00:05:01.934
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yes, okay.

00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:06.439
It's a realty --
it's a realty reality show.

00:05:06.439 --> 00:05:09.742
-Okay, gotcha.
-It's about realtors in L.A.

00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:14.981
And these women, they dress
kind of like anime dinosaurs,

00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:17.950
you know,
like very crazy outfits.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:20.586
And they sell these
huge multimillion-dollar --

00:05:20.586 --> 00:05:22.655
and I'm just laughing,
thinking about if they ever

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:25.391
tried to do that in New York,
like a "Selling Sunset,"

00:05:25.391 --> 00:05:27.727
like "Selling Stuytown,"
you know,

00:05:27.727 --> 00:05:29.962
just the apartments
for the same price

00:05:29.962 --> 00:05:31.496
would just be like a railroad

00:05:31.496 --> 00:05:33.065
with, like, a roommate,
you know?

00:05:33.065 --> 00:05:35.167
And the realtors
wouldn't look like those.

00:05:35.167 --> 00:05:37.036
They'd be, like,
just begrudging, you know,

00:05:37.036 --> 00:05:40.639
"Uh, that's not officially
on the market," you know?

00:05:40.639 --> 00:05:42.742
Just you could never have a show
like that here.

00:05:42.742 --> 00:05:44.277
[ Laughter ]

00:05:44.277 --> 00:05:46.646
-This is a nice milestone,
your son, Gene.

00:05:46.646 --> 00:05:50.215
I've been lucky enough to cross
paths with Gene over the years.

00:05:50.215 --> 00:05:52.452
-You guys see each other
at the gym, mm-hmm.

00:05:52.452 --> 00:05:55.454
-Turning three.
-Turning three tomorrow!

00:05:55.454 --> 00:05:56.789
-Tomorrow!
-My little baby!

00:05:56.789 --> 00:05:59.725
[ Cheers and applause ]
My little baby guy! Yes.

00:05:59.725 --> 00:06:02.662
-Gene, where did the name Gene
come from?

00:06:02.662 --> 00:06:08.167
-Well, actually, Gene was
my husband's mom's name.

00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:11.503
His mom's name was Jean,
and she died, which is sad.

00:06:11.503 --> 00:06:13.238
-Mm-hmm.

00:06:13.238 --> 00:06:14.874
-But to not have
a mother-in-law?

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:18.211
-Right.
[ Light laughter ]

00:06:18.211 --> 00:06:20.012
-I'm sorry!

00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:21.313
Was that a crowd-pleaser?

00:06:21.313 --> 00:06:22.481
-How did you think it would go?

00:06:22.481 --> 00:06:24.316
How did you think it would go?

00:06:24.316 --> 00:06:27.186
-I thought people would be like,
"Oh, that's kind of funny,

00:06:27.186 --> 00:06:29.088
she doesn't have
a mother-in-law."

00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:30.389
I mean, but I guess
you're right.

00:06:30.389 --> 00:06:33.559
I guess, you know --
I think we can all agree

00:06:33.559 --> 00:06:34.926
it would be better
if she were alive.

00:06:34.926 --> 00:06:37.363
-Yes, but --
-Except she's not.

00:06:37.363 --> 00:06:39.699
[ Laughter ]

00:06:39.699 --> 00:06:41.733
No, but, so,
we wanted to honor her.

00:06:41.733 --> 00:06:43.736
We named our son Gene.

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:47.005
And then, as you know, my
husband's last name is Fischer,

00:06:47.005 --> 00:06:48.107
so Gene Fisher.

00:06:48.107 --> 00:06:50.342
Middle name,
named him for Dave Attell.

00:06:50.342 --> 00:06:52.644
So, you know, a month in,

00:06:52.644 --> 00:06:55.814
I realized that I'd named my son
"Genital" Fischer.

00:06:55.814 --> 00:06:57.616
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:57.616 --> 00:07:00.085
Took a full month.
-Gene Attell Fisher, yes.

00:07:00.085 --> 00:07:03.723
-Yeah.
-So, yeah, take it in.

00:07:03.723 --> 00:07:05.958
Take all the time you need.
-It is a hard thing.

00:07:05.958 --> 00:07:08.361
-Let it wash over you
like it did me.

00:07:08.361 --> 00:07:09.495
[ Laughter ]

00:07:09.495 --> 00:07:11.630
-There basically living through,
in a moment,

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:14.399
what took you a full month.
-Right, a month. One month.

00:07:14.399 --> 00:07:17.903
But it wasn't a vulnerable time
at all when you're a new mom.

00:07:17.903 --> 00:07:19.137
-Right, you want to make sure

00:07:19.137 --> 00:07:20.906
you're doing everything perfect
as a new mom.

00:07:20.906 --> 00:07:22.474
And then, 30 days in,
it dawns on you.

00:07:22.474 --> 00:07:24.043
-You just realize
you've already --

00:07:24.043 --> 00:07:25.978
like, has anybody
ever messed up to that level?

00:07:25.978 --> 00:07:28.580
Like, probably not.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:30.850
-But you changed it.
-So we changed it, yeah.

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:32.118
And I hope it's better.

00:07:32.118 --> 00:07:35.888
We changed his middle name
to Dingleberry Fist Crap.

00:07:35.888 --> 00:07:38.357
Is that better, Dingleberry?

00:07:38.357 --> 00:07:40.293
Is that good?
-Weirdly is better.

00:07:40.293 --> 00:07:41.727
[ Laughter ]

00:07:41.727 --> 00:07:44.529
And now this is about
the age introducing him

00:07:44.529 --> 00:07:46.298
to Disney films,
I would imagine.

00:07:46.298 --> 00:07:50.136
-You know, we've been trying,
but they're real problematic.

00:07:50.136 --> 00:07:51.370
Like, I'm sure
you've seen, like,

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:53.305
they have all the warnings now.

00:07:53.305 --> 00:07:55.874
-Yeah, there's, like, a title
card on almost everything.

00:07:55.874 --> 00:07:58.177
-Just like, "We're sorry!

00:07:58.177 --> 00:08:01.013
You know, we're going to still
show it to you," but, um --

00:08:01.013 --> 00:08:03.215
So I tried showing --
I was really excited

00:08:03.215 --> 00:08:05.918
to show him "Peter Pan,"
and we started

00:08:05.918 --> 00:08:08.387
and then like,
you know, not too far in,

00:08:08.387 --> 00:08:11.523
there's, like, an Indian man
in a headdress, like, "ohh!"

00:08:11.523 --> 00:08:14.093
And I'm like, "Okay, no, this is
not good, this is not good."

00:08:14.093 --> 00:08:16.195
[ Laughter ]
And then all the women,

00:08:16.195 --> 00:08:19.731
it's, like, mostly just
those mermaids in "Peter Pan."

00:08:19.731 --> 00:08:22.067
They're topless,
and they all speak in a whisper.

00:08:22.067 --> 00:08:26.439
"Oh, Peter!
Oh, I'm so wet, I'm a mermaid."

00:08:26.439 --> 00:08:28.007
[ Laughter ]

00:08:28.007 --> 00:08:30.609
-Is that actual dialogue?
-It's actual dialogue.

00:08:30.609 --> 00:08:32.011
Check it, check it.

00:08:32.011 --> 00:08:34.580
I'm like, "I don't want my son
to see this," you know?

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:36.949
So we watched "Jungle Book."
Don't even try with that.

00:08:36.949 --> 00:08:39.519
And then so I'm like,
"Let's scrub forward.

00:08:39.519 --> 00:08:40.919
I'm sure the newer ones
are better."

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:43.222
And so we watched --
we wanted to watch "Tangled,"

00:08:43.222 --> 00:08:45.157
which is about Rapunzel.

00:08:45.157 --> 00:08:48.628
And a couple spoilers
are coming your way.

00:08:48.628 --> 00:08:53.532
But in "Tangled,"
if she cuts her hair,

00:08:53.532 --> 00:08:56.769
it turns brown!

00:08:56.769 --> 00:08:58.404
[ Laughter ]

00:08:58.404 --> 00:09:01.240
Yeah, and that happens
in the movie, okay?

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:04.743
And then she has
short brown hair,

00:09:04.743 --> 00:09:06.045
and even though she's hideous,

00:09:06.045 --> 00:09:08.013
the prince still
finds a way to love her.

00:09:08.013 --> 00:09:09.214
-Wow!
-Yeah.

00:09:09.214 --> 00:09:10.449
[ Laughter ]
Isn't that a beautiful message?

00:09:10.449 --> 00:09:12.318
-He puts up with a girl
who has short brown hair?

00:09:12.318 --> 00:09:16.221
-The deformity of brown hair,
yeah.

00:09:16.221 --> 00:09:17.656
It's just --
It's tough, you know?

00:09:17.656 --> 00:09:20.192
These movies -- and I love
the "Monsters, Inc." movies,

00:09:20.192 --> 00:09:21.493
but there's, like, no women.

00:09:21.493 --> 00:09:23.629
You know, it's like,
everybody's got to do better.

00:09:23.629 --> 00:09:25.063
-It's always
so great to see you.

00:09:25.063 --> 00:09:26.031
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:09:26.031 --> 00:09:27.633
You guys, that's Amy Schumer.

00:09:27.633 --> 00:09:30.236
"Life &amp; Beth" is fantastic!

00:09:30.236 --> 00:09:31.937
It's streaming now on Hulu.

00:09:31.937 --> 00:09:33.706
We'll be right back
with Jennifer Grey.

